      {
        "id": 2922366,
        "name": "Lilo Chu",
        "username": "Lilo_Chu",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.eveonline.com/lilo_chu/{size}/510001_2.png",
        "created_at": "2026-09-04T14:47:42.135Z",
        "cooked": "<p>We all know the sense of injustice caused by the existence of multibox players in our beloved EVE Universe.<br>\nTheir capabilities are many times greater than those of single pilots. It is almost impossible to compete with such players. Here are some aspects of anti-multiboxing:</p>\n<p><strong>Unfair advantage / economic imbalance.</strong> A single player fielding several accounts gets what normally requires a whole group of real people — a solo player simply can’t compete with a “fleet” run by one person. In EVE specifically, this shows up as multiboxers clear-cutting an entire asteroid belt with a dozen ships in one pass, and as an outsized share of ISK generation and PLEX-buying that pushes market prices up for everyone else.</p>\n<p><strong>Erosion of the genre’s social core.</strong> MMOs are built around depending on other players, and multiboxing lets someone route around that dependency. Less demand for real teammates means it gets harder for non-boxers to find a group at all. As one forum poster put it, it “forces players to either build a tight-knit real-life group or start boxing themselves.”</p>\n<p><strong>The line between multiboxing and botting.</strong> This is the sharper technical objection: developers can’t reliably tell sophisticated, macro-assisted multiboxing apart from a bot, which makes banning one while allowing the other hard to defend. In EVE’s case, CCP resolved this by banning input-broadcasting software (like ISBoxer) specifically, while leaving manual multiboxing legal.</p>\n<p><strong>Monopolizing content.</strong> A multiboxer can camp a valuable spawn or resource node with several characters at once, locking out anyone who only brought one. EVE’s version of this is players sending suicide-gank alts after anyone who dares mine “their” claimed rocks.</p>\n<p><strong>“If it can be multiboxed, the content is too easy.”</strong> A design critique rather than a player-behavior one: if one unaided person can clear content solo just by running several characters, the content probably wasn’t demanding enough of individual attention to begin with.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantage bought with money, not earned.</strong> Running multiple clients well takes extra subscriptions, extra hardware (or genuinely expensive multi-monitor/multi-PC rigs), and often specialized software — so the edge multiboxing confers is gated by real-world spending power rather than skill or time invested, effectively a pay-to-win dynamic dressed up as a playstyle choice.</p>\n<h2><a name=\"p-2922366-perhaps-its-time-to-civilize-this-content-what-can-be-done-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-2922366-perhaps-its-time-to-civilize-this-content-what-can-be-done-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"></a><strong>Perhaps it’s time to civilize this content? What can be done</strong>:</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Integrate the functionality of third‑party software for multiboxing into the game client itself. Modify the interface for convenient switching between windows.</li>\n<li>Three characters are available for each account. They should become the foundation of the <strong>player‑fleet mechanic</strong>. It is assumed that three pilots can log in simultaneously from one account to pilot and administer them in the game at the same time. Modify the interface to enable convenient management of each pilot and the entire fleet at once in the fleetcom window or in any window of each of the three characters.</li>\n<li>At the same time, to avoid provoking the wrath of the multiboxing community and not to reduce the Company’s financial revenue, preserve the multiboxing capability in the form it currently has.</li>\n<li>To compensate for the Company’s possible financial losses from multiboxing subscriptions, which may decrease with the introduction of the <strong>player‑fleet mechanic</strong>, introduce the corresponding licenses for this mechanic and sell them for PLEX. Undoubtedly, with this approach, sales of licenses for parallel training will increase manyfold.</li>\n<li>With proper technical implementation of the multi‑box client, the hardware requirements will be significantly reduced. The graphical and technical cores of the client will run in a single instance, and when switching between pilots in the <strong>player-fleet</strong>, the add‑ons with data for each pilot and their interface will change, just like in a revolver.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ideas outlined above are just rough outlines of a revolutionary change in approaches to playing EVE. This expansion will make it possible to introduce completely new PvE content with different mechanics, and will also significantly diversify PvP. This will help mitigate the community’s protest against multiboxing and will undoubtedly increase the <strong>company’s real financial revenues</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>I invite all interested pilots to participate in the discussion.</strong></p>\n<p>Sincerely, Lilo Chu<br>\nFly safe.</p>",
